  "_comment": "What each level of detail sets of the fine display arguments of #var, #eq, #verify and #domain (spec docs/requirements/completed/2026-10-21-commandes-typst-refonte.md, REQ-007, ARCH-001). A level is a preset and nothing more: an argument the call writes wins over it, and lib/formula.typ's resolve-display reads the layers in the engineer's order -- the call's argument, the call's level, #calc-defaults of the domain, the domain's level, #calc-defaults of the document, the document's level; --input uniform=N alone overrides the arguments written (Q-005). Each level reproduces what the command printed at that level before the fine arguments existed, so a call written before them prints as it did. Read by Typst alone; tests/test_fine_display.py holds it complete and equal to the table of docs/typst/formulas.md. Since 1.1 a second family, `block` (spec Q-014, 21.10.2026): the same visibility at each level, the label after the line and the citation in the right-hand column, the line set apart -- what `note: \"line\"` printed, chosen for a domain by `#calc-defaults(presets: \"block\", scope: \"domain\")`, which a generated file writes once at its head; `commands` is the default family, `inline`. Since 1.2 (spec 2026-10-22-onglet-neige-type, D-17, ARCH-RISK-F03): `fine-values`, the values each fine argument takes, and `fine-keys`, the fine arguments each command takes -- until then two dictionaries of lib/formula.typ, now read from here by Typst (lib/formula.typ) and by Python (calc/line_display.py: the arguments a line's display is set by, which the engineer's file keeps through an export), so that the two cannot name them two ways.",
